#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# finite_field_log

#% text
# `finite_field_log(a)` gives the discrete logarithm of the nonzero finite field element `a` to the base `x`, the generator: the `k` from 0 to `q - 2` with `x^k == a`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `finite_field_log(a, g)` gives the `k` with `g^k == a`, the least one below the order of `g`, when `a` is a power of `g`; otherwise the call stays unevaluated.

#% item
# The method is Pohlig and Hellman's: the logarithm modulo each prime power dividing the order, by baby steps and giant steps, combined by the Chinese remainder theorem. Prime factors of the order above 2^46 are out of reach.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
F = finite_field(11, 3)

#% cell
a = F([3, 1, 4])

#% cell
k = finite_field_log(a)

#% cell
F([0, 1])^k == a
#% output
#= true

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Another base:

#% cell
g = F([2, 2])

#% cell
finite_field_log(g^100, g)
#% output
#= 100

#% text
# A larger field:

#% cell
G = finite_field(2, 40)

#% cell
finite_field_log(G([0, 1])^123456789012)
#% output
#= 123456789012

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The logarithm inverts the power, and for the exponential representation it is the shown index less one:

#% cell
Q = finite_field(7, 2)
k = finite_field_log(Q([3, 5]))
[Q([0, 1])^k == Q([3, 5]), finite_field_log(finite_field(7, 2, "Exponential")(10)), finite_field_log(Q(6))]
#% output
#= [true, 9, 24]

#% text
# A field with large characteristic (about 10^12 elements):

#% cell
big = finite_field(1000003, 2)
big([0, 1])^finite_field_log(big([5, 7])) == big([5, 7])
#% output
#= true

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# Zero has no logarithm, and an element that is not a power of the base gives no answer (the middle value is 0: `1` is the 0th power):

#% cell
[finite_field_log(Q(0)), finite_field_log(Q(1), Q([0, 1])^2), finite_field_log(Q([0, 1]), Q([0, 1])^2)]
#% output
#= [finite_field_log(finite_field_element(finite_field(7, &(3 + 6*&1 + &1^2), "Polynomial"), [])), 0, finite_field_log(finite_field_element(finite_field(7, &(3 + 6*&1 + &1^2), "Polynomial"), [0, 1]), finite_field_element(finite_field(7, &(3 + 6*&1 + &1^2), "Polynomial"), [4, 1]))]

#% text
# The logarithm is the least one: the base `3` has the order 6 in `F_49`, so `3^7 = 3`:

#% cell
finite_field_log(Q(3)^7, Q(3))
#% output
#= 1

